cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: >-
  sitediverse: optical ground-station site-diversity availability under
  measured spatial correlation, against the independence assumption that
  overstates it
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-11-10
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  Measures how much the independence assumption overstates the availability of
  an optical ground-station diversity network. Baseline 1 is the naive product
  of marginal cloud probabilities, implemented deliberately as the thing being
  refuted because it is what site-diversity practice does; it is computed in
  logarithmic space so that the complement never loses its digits. Baseline 2 is
  a Gaussian copula with a fitted two-parameter exponential correlation-length
  model over station separation, with a strengthened per-regime form reported
  beside it; the bivariate orthant is evaluated through the Plackett reduction
  formula, so the correlation excess is an integral of a positive integrand
  rather than a subtraction of two nearly equal probabilities and keeps full
  relative precision down to a correlation of 1e-20, where the subtraction
  returns exactly zero for a quantity of 1.964128e-25. Baseline 3 is the direct
  empirical joint availability from the concurrent record with moving-block
  bootstrap confidence intervals. A learned spatio-temporal correlation model
  with a quantile-regression interval output and no bare point-estimate method
  is scored on held-out SITES and held-out TIME separately and never merged. The
  climatology sampling uncertainty is propagated and reported beside every
  availability figure. The headline is the overstatement: on a declared
  six-station network the independence assumption claims a joint unavailability
  of 0.000866 plus or minus 0.000037 where the record shows 0.019906 plus or
  minus 0.001472, an overstatement factor of 22.9809 and 1.3614 nines of
  availability that are not there; on a four-station network inside one regional
  group the factor is 14.2124 and 1.1527 nines. An exact decomposition separates
  what assuming a stationary climatology costs from what assuming spatial
  independence costs, with a residual of zero on every network, and two of six
  networks have a negative co-modulation term and an overstatement ratio below
  one. The learned result is the research finding and it is reported plainly: on
  every hold-out cell of both declared records the learned margin over the
  strongest baseline is smaller than the bootstrap half-width on the empirical
  reference; on held-out TIME the fitted correlation-length baseline beats the
  learned model on both records, by 0.003459 and 0.003168 in mean absolute error
  of joint unavailability; on the record where the baseline is correctly
  specified by construction the held-out-SITES margin of 0.000764 has a paired
  bootstrap interval of minus 0.000604 to plus 0.001409, which contains zero;
  and scored against the generator's own correlation rather than against the
  noisy empirical target the learned model is worse than the baseline even on
  the cell it wins, 0.063107 against 0.043236, because it is fitting the
  estimation noise in a target whose own root-mean-square error against the
  truth is 0.073751. Validation is level 3, with 433 tests counted from junit
  XML, 26 negative controls of which 15 must fire and all 15 do and 11 must not
  and none do, an audit finding 0 of 433 assertions dependent on the hidden
  pytest.approx absolute default, 0 non-zero exits in 66 working-directory probe
  runs, and checks against mathematics the implementation does not use including
  the Frechet-Hoeffding bounds with 0 violations in 380 comparisons and an exact
  two-station inclusion-exclusion identity closing to 2.220446e-16 over all 190
  pairs. All numbers are measured against a synthetic concurrent cloud record
  generated by the package's own declared equations from a fixed seed, with a
  known spatial correlation length; no real site climatology is used and no real
  optical ground station is a data source, which is stated at length in the
  dataset card because a model that recovers its own generator has demonstrated
  consistency and not skill. Research grade. Not flight-qualified, not
  certified, not approved for operational aerospace use.
authors:
  - name: "OPTIMA Organisation"
keywords:
  - site-diversity
  - optical-ground-station
  - free-space-optical
  - cloud-climatology
  - gaussian-copula
  - correlation-length
  - block-bootstrap
  - frechet-hoeffding
  - availability
references:
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Sklar
        given-names: "A."
    title: >-
      Fonctions de repartition a n dimensions et leurs marges
    journal: >-
      Publications de l'Institut de Statistique de l'Universite de Paris
    volume: 8
    year: 1959
    start: 229
    end: 231
    notes: >-
      The theorem that a joint distribution with continuous margins factors
      into its margins and a unique copula. Cited for the statement AND for its
      discrete-margin caveat: for a binary cloud indicator the copula is not
      unique, so equation (8) asserts a latent-variable model rather than a
      consequence of the theorem, and README.md names that as the first thing to
      doubt on a real record.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Nelsen
        given-names: "R. B."
    title: "An Introduction to Copulas"
    edition: "2nd"
    publisher:
      name: "Springer"
    year: 2006
    notes: >-
      Chapter 2 for Sklar's theorem and the non-uniqueness of the copula for
      discrete margins; theorem 2.10.12 for the Frechet-Hoeffding bounds as the
      copulas W and M, which is the bound this product checks every route
      against and which it measures 0 violations of in 380 comparisons.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Plackett
        given-names: "R. L."
    title: "A reduction formula for normal multivariate integrals"
    journal: "Biometrika"
    volume: 41
    year: 1954
    start: 351
    end: 360
    notes: >-
      The reduction formula behind equation (9). Its consequence here is that
      the correlation excess is an integral of a positive integrand and never a
      subtraction: measured, the integral form keeps full relative precision at
      a correlation of 1e-20 where the subtraction returns exactly 0.0.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Genz
        given-names: "A."
    title: >-
      Numerical computation of rectangular bivariate and trivariate normal and
      t probabilities
    journal: "Statistics and Computing"
    volume: 14
    year: 2004
    start: 251
    end: 260
    notes: >-
      The algorithm SciPy's multivariate normal CDF implements. Used ONLY as an
      independent cross-check in validation/validate_copula.py and by no module
      under src/; the two implementations agree to 1.088539e-16 absolute over
      600 cases.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Efron
        given-names: "B."
      - family-names: Tibshirani
        given-names: "R. J."
    title: "An Introduction to the Bootstrap"
    publisher:
      name: "Chapman and Hall"
    year: 1993
    notes: >-
      Chapter 13 for the percentile interval attached to every empirical
      availability figure here.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: "Kunsch"
        given-names: "H. R."
    title: >-
      The jackknife and the bootstrap for general stationary observations
    journal: "Annals of Statistics"
    volume: 17
    issue: 3
    year: 1989
    start: 1217
    end: 1241
    notes: >-
      The moving-block bootstrap of equation (21). Necessary here because the
      record's epochs are serially dependent: measured, the block bootstrap's
      standard error exceeds the iid bootstrap's by a factor of 1.2992 to
      2.3479 across the declared networks.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Stein
        given-names: "M. L."
    title: "Interpolation of Spatial Data"
    publisher:
      name: "Springer"
    year: 1999
    notes: >-
      Chapter 2 for the Matern family. The exponential correlation of equation
      (4) is Matern at smoothness one half, and the same chapter gives the
      positive-definiteness result that does NOT extend to a great-circle
      distance, which is why correlation.matrix_from_pairs projects and reports
      the projection.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Friedman
        given-names: "J. H."
    title: "Greedy function approximation: a gradient boosting machine"
    journal: "Annals of Statistics"
    volume: 29
    issue: 5
    year: 2001
    start: 1189
    end: 1232
    notes: >-
      The algorithm behind sklearn.ensemble.GradientBoostingRegressor, which
      fits the learned component's three quantile models.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Bartlett
        given-names: "M. S."
    title: >-
      On the theoretical specification of sampling properties of autocorrelated
      time series
    journal: "Journal of the Royal Statistical Society B"
    volume: 8
    issue: 1
    year: 1946
    start: 27
    end: 41
    notes: >-
      The effective sample size of equation (13). Measured here to disagree with
      a block bootstrap on 20 of 20 stations by more than 5 per cent, which is
      reported rather than claimed away: the AR(1) correction understates the
      inflation because a thresholded persistent Gaussian is not an AR(1).
  - type: standard
    title: >-
      ITU-R Recommendation P.618: Propagation data and prediction methods
      required for the design of Earth-space telecommunication systems
    institution:
      name: "International Telecommunication Union, Radiocommunication Sector"
    notes: >-
      Cited for the framework and NOT for any clause or numerical value, none of
      which is used or reproduced here: the Earth-space propagation and
      site-diversity material that parameterises diversity statistics by station
      separation, and that treats an independence assumption as optimistic.
  - type: standard
    title: >-
      ITU-R Recommendation P.1815: Differential rain attenuation
    institution:
      name: "International Telecommunication Union, Radiocommunication Sector"
    notes: >-
      Cited for the same reason and with the same restriction: the
      separation-parameterised treatment of site diversity, including the
      dependence on the orientation of the baseline relative to the prevailing
      storm direction, which is the physical motivation for the along-axis and
      cross-axis decomposition of equation (3). No clause and no number from it
      is reproduced here.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Andrews
        given-names: "L. C."
      - family-names: Phillips
        given-names: "R. L."
    title: "Laser Beam Propagation through Random Media"
    edition: "2nd"
    publisher:
      name: "SPIE Press"
    year: 2005
    notes: >-
      Cited for the propagation physics this package does NOT model:
      scintillation, fade statistics and aperture averaging. A station here is
      up or down according to a binary cloud indicator and nothing else.
  - type: conference-paper
    authors:
      - family-names: Kim
        given-names: "I. I."
      - family-names: McArthur
        given-names: "B."
      - family-names: Korevaar
        given-names: "E."
    title: >-
      Comparison of laser beam propagation at 785 nm and 1550 nm in fog and
      haze for optical wireless communications
    collection-title: "Proceedings of SPIE vol. 4214, Optical Wireless Communications III"
    year: 2001
    notes: >-
      Cited, like Andrews and Phillips, for the atmospheric attenuation physics
      outside this package's scope. Nothing from it is reproduced or compared
      against.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Fuchs
        given-names: "C."
      - family-names: Giggenbach
        given-names: "D."
    title: >-
      Optical free-space laser communication for satellite downlinks and the
      role of ground-station site diversity
    year: 2012
    notes: >-
      Cited for the engineering context: that cloud blockage is the dominant
      availability driver for an optical satellite downlink and that ground
      station diversity is the standard mitigation. No number from it is
      reproduced here, and this product measures the error of an assumption
      rather than reproducing any published availability figure.
